## Coding Standards

### Python (Backend)
- Use `async def` for all route handlers and service functions
- Type hints on ALL function signatures — no exceptions
- Pydantic models for ALL API request/response schemas
- Use `from __future__ import annotations` in every file
- Error handling: catch specific exceptions, log them, return structured error responses
- Logging: use `structlog` or stdlib `logging` — log every pipeline step
- Never hardcode API keys — always from `config.py` which reads `.env`
- Amount handling: Razorpay uses PAISE (integer). Always convert:
  `amount_rupees = amount_paise / 100`

### React (Frontend)
- Functional components only, hooks for state
- TailwindCSS for all styling — no CSS files
- Use a clean, professional color scheme: slate grays + blue accent
- Loading states on every data fetch
- Error states with clear messages
- Responsive but desktop-first (judges will see it on a laptop)

### General
- No comments explaining obvious code. Comments only for WHY, not WHAT.
- No placeholder/TODO code. Every function must be fully implemented.
- No print statements — use proper logging.
- Git commits after each completed task with descriptive messages.

## Razorpay API Notes (CRITICAL)
- ALWAYS use test mode API keys (start with `rzp_test_`)
- Razorpay amounts are in PAISE (₹100 = 10000 paise)
- Dispute Contest API: `PATCH /v1/disputes/{id}` with evidence fields + `action: "submit"`
- Document Upload API: `POST /v1/documents` with `purpose: "dispute_evidence"`
- Webhook event for disputes: `payment.dispute.created`
- Dispute evidence fields: shipping_proof, billing_proof, customer_communication,
  proof_of_service, explanation_letter, refund_confirmation, access_activity_log,
  refund_cancellation_policy, term_and_conditions, others
- Each evidence field takes an array of document IDs (from Document Upload API)
- `summary` field: max 1000 characters, text explanation
- Contest can be partial (specify `amount`) or full (omit `amount`)

## LLM Integration Notes
- Kimi API via NVIDIA NIM inference endpoint
- Endpoint URL and API key from environment variables
- Model: configure via env var (default to whatever Kimi model is available on NIM)
- The ONLY LLM call in the entire system is `generate_explanation_letter()`
- Prompt must include: reason code, payment details, order details, shipping status,
  refund status — structured as context, then instruction
- Output: a formal, professional explanation letter (500-800 words)
- Temperature: 0.3 (we want consistent, professional output, not creative)
- Include a system prompt that sets the role as a dispute resolution specialist

## What NOT to Build
- No authentication/login system
- No user management
- No payment processing (we only READ payment data)
- No real-time WebSocket updates (polling is fine for a hackathon)
- No mobile responsiveness (desktop-first)
- No unit tests (time constraint — focus on working demo)
- No Docker/containerization (localhost is fine)
- No multi-agent/LangChain/CrewAI — this is a simple pipeline
